description: "Write a function called `count_tribal_control` that analyzes village\
  \ control in a linear arrangement.\n\nGiven a string representing villages in a\
  \ line, where:\n- 'A' represents a village occupied by tribe A\n- 'B' represents\
  \ a village occupied by tribe B  \n- '.' represents an empty village\n\nAn empty\
  \ village is controlled by a tribe if it is surrounded by villages of that tribe\
  \ from both the left and right sides. A village occupied by a tribe is also counted\
  \ as being controlled by that tribe.\n\nYour function should return a tuple of two\
  \ integers: (count_A, count_B) where count_A is the number of villages either occupied\
  \ by or controlled by tribe A, and count_B is the number of villages either occupied\
  \ by or controlled by tribe B.\n\n**Function signature:** `def count_tribal_control(villages:\
  \ str) -> tuple[int, int]:`\n\n**Examples:**\n- `count_tribal_control(\"A..A..B...B\"\
  )` returns `(4, 5)`\n  - Tribe A: positions 0, 1, 2, 3 (A + controlled .. + A)\n\
  \  - Tribe B: positions 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 (B + controlled ... + B)\n\n- `count_tribal_control(\"\
  ..A..\")` returns `(1, 0)`\n  - Only the A at position 2 is controlled by tribe\
  \ A\n\n- `count_tribal_control(\"A....A\")` returns `(6, 0)`\n  - All 6 positions\
  \ are controlled by tribe A (A + controlled .... + A)\n\n- `count_tribal_control(\"\
  ..B..B..B..\")` returns `(0, 7)`\n  - Tribe B controls positions 2-8 (B + controlled\
  \ .. + B + controlled .. + B)\n\n**Constraints:**\n- 1 ≤ len(villages) ≤ 10^5\n\
  - villages contains only 'A', 'B', and '.' characters"
